<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	CoreTend is NOT App Sandboxed. Its core function — finding and cleaning
	duplicate/large/leftover files anywhere the user points it, inspecting
	other applications' support data for uninstall leftovers, and probing
	TCC-protected locations to detect Full Disk Access — is fundamentally
	incompatible with the App Sandbox's per-app container model. This is
	the same distribution model used by comparable Mac cleanup utilities
	outside the Mac App Store. See Documentation/SIGNING_NOTARIZATION.md.

	Hardened Runtime is still enabled (required for notarization) with the
	narrowest possible entitlement set: only what the app actually does.
	Nothing here is added speculatively.
	-->

	<!-- Runs signed binaries the app itself does not control the format of:
	     Process spawns clamscan (Homebrew/MacPorts binary, code-signed by
	     its own installer or unsigned entirely). Hardened Runtime's default
	     library-validation would refuse to load code from these paths only
	     if CoreTend loaded them as a library; it doesn't — clamscan runs as
	     a separate process via Process/NSTask, which Hardened Runtime does
	     not restrict. No entitlement is required for this and none is
	     requested. This entitlement set intentionally stays empty of
	     com.apple.security.cs.* exceptions: no disabled library validation,
	     no unsigned executable memory, no debugging, no DYLD environment
	     variables. -->
</dict>
</plist>
